t split uses the same logic (e.g., a user-level split with at least one test interaction per user where possible) and log the split details per seed.\\n\\n3) Model and hyperparameters\\n- Hyperparameter exposure: Expose alpha, lambda, n_factors, max_epochs/iterations, and set a fixed random_state/seed. The code fixes a few but omits alpha and lambda in the current ImplicitMF configuration. Use a configurable interface so all runs share the same values except for seed.\\n- Binarization impact: Because we binarize, you may also need to adjust how ImplicitMF interprets confidence (alpha) and ensure the model initialization is seeded.\\n\\n4) Training and evaluation workflow\\n- Candidate item filtering: You correctly exclude train-visible items, but document and code a robust path for users with no test interactions (drop from evaluation gracefully).\\n- Scoring stability: If LensKit isn’t available, the code falls back to random scores. For reproducibility, you should either require LensKit in the environment or implement a deterministic emulator consistent with the thresholded implicit representation.\\n\\n5) Metrics and reporting\\n- Aggregation: Compute per-seed metrics and then aggregate across seeds (mean, std) and optionally perform a paired t-test. The code currently prints per-seed metrics and saves per-seed results but doesn’t clearly write a final aggregated CSV for all seeds.\\n- Output formats: Save a final aggregated results file (CSV) and, if desired, a per-seed file with per-user metrics. Also print concise summary (seed, ndcg@10, recall@10) with means and stds.\\n\\n6) Reproducibility and logging\\n- Logging scope: Log the exact train/test split (e.g., indices or item IDs per user) and hyperparameters for each seed. Persist model/hyperparameter state as needed.\\n- Robust failure handling: If a seed yields an unusable split, the run should be skipped with a logged note, preserving other seeds’ results.\\n\\n7) Output format and persistence\\n- Ensure final results are produced in reusable formats: results_all_seeds.csv with seed, ndcg_10, recall_10, and per-seed detailed files if desired.\\n- Persist internal mappings and version information to enable full reproducibility.\\n\\n8) Environment and compatibility notes\\n- Document dependencies (LensKit version, Python version) and ensure the environment used for evaluation is consistent. If LensKit isn’t installed, document the fallback behavior and consider tightening to rely on a reproducible simulated environment.\\n\\nOverall, the implementation is a solid starting point but requires completing data preprocessing (binaraization and ID mapping), expanding seeds to the predetermined set, fully exposing hyperparameters, robust logging, and reproducible result persistence to fully satisfy the experiment design."}', name='score_code'), type='function')]))[/cyan]
