tion: Compute per-seed metrics and then aggregate across seeds (mean, std) and optionally perform a paired t-test. The code currently prints per-seed metrics and saves per-seed results but doesn’t clearly write a final aggregated CSV for all seeds.\n- Output formats: Save a final aggregated results file (CSV) and, if desired, a per-seed file with per-user metrics. Also print concise summary (seed, ndcg@10, recall@10) with means and stds.\n\n6) Reproducibility and logging\n- Logging scope: Log the exact train/test split (e.g., indices or item IDs per user) and hyperparameters for each seed. Persist model/hyperparameter state as needed.\n- Robust failure handling: If a seed yields an unusable split, the run should be skipped with a logged note, preserving other seeds’ results.\n\n7) Output format and persistence\n- Ensure final results are produced in reusable formats: results_all_seeds.csv with seed, ndcg_10, recall_10, and per-seed detailed files if desired.\n- Persist internal mappings and version information to enable full reproducibility.\n\n8) Environment and compatibility notes\n- Document dependencies (LensKit version, Python version) and ensure the environment used for evaluation is consistent. If LensKit isn’t installed, document the fallback behavior and consider tightening to rely on a reproducible simulated environment.\n\nOverall, the implementation is a solid starting point but requires completing data preprocessing (binaraization and ID mapping), expanding seeds to the predetermined set, fully exposing hyperparameters, robust logging, and reproducible result persistence to fully satisfy the experiment design.\n                ', 'Feedback about execution time': '', 'Instructions': {'Response format': 'Your response should be a brief outline/sketch of your proposed solution in natural language (7-10 sentences), followed by a single markdown code block (using the format ```python ... ```) which implements this solution and prints out the evaluation metri