[2026/05/13 17:34:03] [INFO] isgsa.nodeAgent: Node identified as buggy: Detected a bug in the evaluation setup: The recall@K and ndcg@K calculations are performed by ranking over a candidate set that consists only of the test interactions with all predicted scores set to a constant (0.0). This causes every user to have top-K predictions drawn from their test items and yields Recall@K = 1 and NDCG@K = 1 (assuming len(test) >= K), which is an artificial, misleading signal of model performance. The result is not a valid measure of how seeds affect model outcomes since the evaluation setup itself guarantees perfect scores.

Root cause:
- Test-set items are used as the candidate pool for ranking with identical scores, so the top-K predictions are always the first K test items per user, which are all relevant by construction.

Fix suggestions:
1) Generate a proper candidate set per user that includes negative samples (items not observed in training). Typically, sample a fixed number of negative items per user (e.g., 100–1000) and use all observed test items as positives.
2) Train the model on the training split, then score each candidate item for each user (both positives and negatives).
3) Compute Recall@K and NDCG@K against the ground-truth test interactions using the ranked scores, ensuring that the evaluation uses all required candidates rather than only test items with uniform scores.
4) Consider separate reporting of seed-to-seed variability with consistent candidate sampling.

Optional enhancements:
- Use LensKit's built-in evaluation utilities or implement a per-user candidate sampler to ensure reproducible evaluation.
- Record per-seed results and aggregate with std deviation to quantify seed sensitivity.
