y a single markdown code block (using the format ```python ... ```) which implements this solution and prints out the evaluation metric(s) if applicable. There should be no additional headings or text in your response. Just natural language text followed by a newline and then the markdown code block. Make sure to write concise code.', 'Improvement guidelines': ['Based on the scoring feedback above, focus on the requirements that need improvement.', 'Your goal is to enhance the implementation while maintaining its working functionality.'], 'Implementation guideline': ['CRITICAL REQUIREMENTS - You should use lenskit as much as possible', 'CRITICAL MODEL INPUT GUIDELINES:', '  - Always pay extra attention to the input to the model being properly normalized', "  - This is extremely important because the input to the model's forward pass directly affects the output, and the loss function is computed based on the output", 'For generative modeling tasks, you must:', '  - Generate a set of samples from your model', '  - Compare these samples with ground truth data using appropriate visualizations', "  - When saving plots, always use the 'working_dir' variable that will be defined at the start of the script", '  - Make sure to give each figure a unique and appropriate name based on the dataset it represents, rather than reusing the same filename.', 'Important code structure requirements:', '  - Do NOT put any execution code inside \'if __name__ == "__main__":\' block', '  - All code should be at the global scope or in functions that are called from the global scope', '  - The script should execute immediately when run, without requiring any special entry point', 'The code should start with:', '  import os', "  working_dir = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), 'working')", '  os.makedirs(working_dir, exist_ok=True)', 'The code should be a single-file python program that is self-contained and can be executed as-is.', "No parts of the code should be skipped, don't terminate the code execution before finishing the script.", 'Your response should only contain a single code block.', 'Be aware of the running time of the code, it should complete within an hour.', 'You can also use the "./working" directory to store any temporary files that your code needs to create.', 'Data saving requirements:', '- Save all plottable data (metrics, losses, predictions, etc.) as numpy arrays using np.save()', '- Use the following naming convention for saved files:', '  ```python', '  # At the start of your code', '  experiment_data = {', "      'dataset_name_1': {", "          'metrics': {'train': [], 'val': []},", "          'losses': {'train': [], 'val': []},", "          'predictions': [],", "          'ground_truth': [],", '          # Add other relevant data', '      },', '      # Add additional datasets as needed:', "      'dataset_name_2': {", "          'metrics': {'train': [], 'val': []},", "          'losses': {'train': [], 'val': []},", "          'predictions': [],", "          'ground_truth': [],", '          # Add other relevant data', '      },', '  }', '  # During training/evaluation:', "  experiment_data['dataset_name_1']['metrics']['train'].append(train_metric)", '  ```', '- Include timestamps or epochs with the saved metrics', '- For large datasets, consider saving in chunks or using np.savez_compressed()', 'CRITICAL EVALUATION REQUIREMENTS - Your code MUST include ALL of these:', '  1. Track and print validation loss (if applicable) at each epoch or at suitable intervals:', '     ```python', "     print(f'Epoch {{epoch}}: validation_loss = {{val_loss:.4f}}')", '     ```', '  2. Track and update ALL these additional metrics: None', '  3. Update metrics at EACH epoch:', '  4. Save ALL metrics at the end:', '     ```python', "     np.save(os.path.join(working_dir, 'experiment_data.npy'), experiment_data)", '     ```']}, 'Previous solution': {'Code': '```python\nimport os\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport random\nfrom math import log2\n\n# Setup working directory\nworking_dir = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \'working\')\nos.makedirs(working_dir, exist_ok=True)\n\n# Load MovieLens 100K data from current directory\nDATA_PATH = \'u.data\'\nif not os.path.exists(DATA_PATH):\n    raise SystemExit("u.data not found in current directory. Please place MovieLens100K u.data in the working directory.")\n\n# Load, select implicit interactions (rating >= 4), and convert to 0-based IDs\ncol_names = [\'user\', \'item\', \'rating\', \'timestamp\']\ndf = pd.read_csv(DATA_PATH, sep=\'\\t\', header=None, names=col_names, engine=\'python\')\ndf = df[df[\'rating\'] >= 4].copy()\ndf[\'user\'] = df[\'user\'].astype(int) - 1\ndf[\'item\'] = df[\'item\'].astype(int) - 1\nn_users = int(df[\'user\'].max()) + 1\nn_items = int(df[\'item\'].max()) + 1\n\n# Global experiment settings\nSEEDS = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\nEPOCHS = 5\nK = 20  # latent factors\nLR = 0.05\nREG = 0.01\nTEST_FRAC = 0.2\nTOP_K = 10  # evaluation cutoff\n\n# Helper utilities\nsigmoid = lambda x: 1.0 / (1.0 + np.exp(-x))\n\n# Prepare a container for experiment data\nexperiment_key = \'MovieLens100K_ImplicitMF\'\nexperiment_data = {\n    experiment_key: {\n        \'metrics\': {\'val\': []},  # per-epoch val loss\n        \'losses\': {\'train\': [], \'val\': []},  # per-epoch training and val losses\n        \'predictions\': [],  # per-seed per-epoch predictions (optional per-epoch snapshot)\n        \'ground_truth\': [],  # per-seed ground truth info\n        \'seed\': None,\n    }\n}\n\n# Utility to split per-user train/test\ndef per_seed_split(df_seed, test_frac=TEST_FRAC):\n    train_pairs = []\n    test_pairs = []\n    gt_per_user = {}\n    for u, group in df_seed.groupby(\'user\'):\n        items = group[\'item\'].values\n        if len(items) == 0:\n            continue\n        n_test = max(1, int(len(items) * test_frac))\n        # deterministic shuffle per seed using numpy RNG seeded outside\n        idx = np.random.permutation(len(items))\n        test_idx = idx[:n_test]\n        train_idx = idx[n_test:]\n        test_items = items[test_idx]\n        train_items = items[train_idx]\n        for it in train_items:\n            train_pairs.append((int(u), int(it)))\n        for it in test_items:\n            test_pairs.append((int(u), int(it)))\n            gt_per_user.setdefault(int(u), set()).add(int(it))\n    return train_pairs, test_pairs, gt_per_user\n\n# Training loop (simple SGD for implicit MF)\ndef train_implicit_mf(train_pairs, n_users, n_items, seed):\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    U = rng.normal(scale=0.1, size=(n_users, K))\n    V = rng.normal(scale=0.1, size=(n_items, K))\n    epoch_losses = []\n    for epoch in range(EPOCHS):\n        # shuffle training pairs\n        order = np.arange(len(train_pairs))\n        rng.shuffle(order)\n        train_loss = 0.0\n        for idx in order:\n            u, i = train_pairs[idx]\n            u_vec = U[u]\n            v_vec = V[i]\n            score = u_vec @ v_vec\n            p = sigmoid(score)\n            grad = p - 1.0  # derivative for positive sample\n            old_u = u_vec.copy()\n            U[u] -= LR * (grad * v_vec + REG * u_vec)\n            V[i] -= LR * (grad * old_u + REG * v_vec)\n            # logistic loss for this positive sample\n            train_loss += -np.log(p + 1e-12)\n        train_loss /= max(1, len(train_pairs))\n        epoch_losses.append(train_loss)\n    return U, V, epoch_losses\n\n# Evaluation metrics (NDCG@K and Recall@K)\n\ndef evaluate_metrics(U, V, test_pairs, gt_per_user, K_eval=TOP_K):\n    # Build per-user ground truth for test users\n    users_with_gt = list(gt_per_user.keys())\n    if not users_with_gt:\n        return 0.0, 0.0\n    total_ndcg = 0.0\n    total_recall = 0.0\n    count_users = 0\n    for u in users_with_gt:\n        # scores for all items for this user\n        scores = V @ U[u]  # shape (n_items,)\n        top_indices = np.argsort(scores)[-K_eval:][::-1]\n        gt = gt_per_user[u]\n        if len(gt) == 0:\n            continue\n        # compute DCG for binary relevances\n        dcg = 0.0\n        for rank, idx in enumerate(top_indices, start=1):\n            rel = 1 if int(idx) in gt else 0\n            if rel:\n                dcg += 1.0 / log2(rank + 1)\n        idcg = sum(1.0 / log2(r + 1) for r in range(1, min(K_eval, len(gt)) + 1))\n        ndcg = dcg / max(idcg, 1e-12)\n        recall = len(gt.intersection(set(top_indices))) / float(len(gt))\n        total_ndcg += ndcg\n        total_recall += recall\n        count_users += 1\n    if count_users == 0:\n        return 0.0, 0.0\n    return total_ndcg / count_users, total_recall / count_users\n\n# Main loop over seeds\nfor seed in SEEDS:\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    random.seed(seed)\n    # Split data per seed\n    train_pairs, test_pairs, gt_per_user = per_seed_split(df, TEST_FRAC)\n\n    # Train model\n    U, V, epoch_loss_list = train_implicit_mf(train_pairs, n_users, n_items, seed)\n\n    # Validation after final epoch\n    # compute val_loss on test set\n    val_loss_total = 0.0\n    for (u, i) in test_pairs:\n        score = U[u] @ V[i]\n        p = sigmoid(score)\n        val_loss_total += -np.log(p + 1e-12)\n    val_loss = val_loss_total / max(1, len(test_pairs))\n\n    # Evaluate ranking metrics on test set\n    ndcg, recall = evaluate_metrics(U, V, test_pairs, gt_per_user, K_eval=TOP_K)\n\n    # Save per-seed results\n    experiment_data[experiment_key][\'metrics\'][\'val\'].append((seed, val_loss, ndcg, recall))\n    experiment_data[experiment_key][\'losses\'][\'train\'].append((seed, epoch_loss_list))\n    experiment_data[experiment_key][\'predictions\'].append({})\n    experiment_data[experiment_key][\'ground_truth\'].append({})\n    experiment_data[experiment_key][\'seed\'] = seed\n\n    print(f\'Seed {seed}: val_loss={val_loss:.4f}, ndcg@{TOP_K}={ndcg:.4f}, recall@{TOP_K}={recall:.4f}\')\n\n# Persist experiment data\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, \'experiment_data.npy\'), experiment_data)\nprint(\'Experiment completed. Data saved to\', os.path.join(working_dir, \'experiment_data.npy\'))\n\n```'}}
