r user by reinitializing RandomState(seed) for each user; while deterministic, this yields a potentially biased repeatability pattern across users. It would be more robust to use a single RNG per run and sample per-user holdout with that RNG.\\n- You do not explicitly drop users with insufficient interactions in train or test (the comment mentions it, but the code doesn’t implement a hard threshold). Implement a minimum interaction threshold, and drop users who do not meet it, with logging.\\n\\n3) Repeated experiments with seeds\\n- This part is implemented: you iterate over SEEDS, reconstruct the train/test split from the original data per seed, and run the model. This is good for reproducibility.\\n\\n4) Model configuration (ImplicitMF)\\n- You set n_factors and n_epochs, but you do not expose a configurable lambda (regularization) constant. The requirement specifies a fixed lambda=0.01 configurable.\\n- Initialization/determinism: The code passes seed to ImplicitMF; ensure that initialization is deterministic by explicitly seeding any RNG used by the implementation (LensKit may seed internally, but document or enforce it).\\n- Ensure you set hyperparameters consistently across seeds (which you do for n_factors and n_epochs).\\n\\n5) Evaluation protocol and metrics\\n- You only compute NDCG@K and Recall@K for K=10. The requirement asks for K in {5,10,20}. You should compute metrics for all three K values.\\n- Also ensure evaluation excludes users with no test items and handles users with no recommendations gracefully.\\n\\n6) Result aggregation and analysis\\n- The code currently saves a numpy file with per-seed metrics but does not compute mean/std across seeds, nor does it emit a CSV like results_seeded.csv or a summary file. You should aggregate metrics across seeds and store both a per-seed table and a summary of mean±std per (metric, K).\\n\\n7) Outputs and logging\\n- You should output: a CSV results_seeded.csv with columns: seed, K, metric_name, value; and a summary_seeded.csv with mean and std per metric and K. Also log seeds used and per-run runtime.\\n\\n8) Reproducibility and determinism constraints\\n- You seed per run, but the per-user sampling should be driven by a single per-run RNG, not reinitialized RandomState(seed) for every user. Also ensure all library RNGs are seeded (e.g., Python random and NumPy) for full determinism.\\n\\n9) Robustness and edge-case handling\\n- If a user has zero interactions in training after the split, you should drop that user or skip evaluation and log the reason. Your current approach may produce empty predictions for some users; ensure that test users exist and handle edge cases gracefully.\\n\\n10) Practical constraints and docs\\n- The solution is not yet modular/configurable via a small config (command-line or dict) for seeds, f, n_factors, lambda, n_epochs, K values, alpha. The current constants make experimentation tedious. Add a config section or argparse to set these without editing code. Include a concise README documenting experiment design, seeds rationale, and how to reproduce.\\n\\nRecommendations for a solid next iteration:\\n- Implement a proper implicit pipeline: read u.data, map IDs to 0-based indexing, compute confidence = 1 + alpha * rating, and pass a dataset with (user, item, confidence) to ImplicitMF.\\n- Add a parameterized holdout fraction f (e.g., f=0.2), using a single RNG per run to sample test items per user. Drop users who fail the threshold (e.g., less than min_interactions) and log the drop.\\n- Ensure the Hyperparameters include lambda=0.01 (configurable) and fix a deterministic initialization with the per-run seed.\\n- Extend evaluation to K in {5,10,20} and compute NDCG@K and Recall@K for all three values. Exclude users with no test data or zero predictions gracefully.\\n- After running all seeds, compute per-metric mean and std across seeds, and write both a per-seed results file and a summary file (CSV). Add runtime logging.\\n- Add a compact README near the script explaining experiment design, seeds rationale, and how to reproduce.\\n\\nIf you want, I can provide a minimal refactor plan or a drop-in rewritten script skeleton that implements all of the above."}', name='score_code'), type='function')]))[/cyan]
