ned and used to reproduce splits. (Req 3)\n- Evaluation protocol: NDCG@K and Recall@K are computed on the test set for K in {5,10,20}. (Req 5)\n- Reproducibility focus: Seeding is applied for splits and model initialization to some degree. (Req 8)\n- Robustness around user filtering: Users with insufficient interactions are dropped, avoiding undefined evaluation. (Req 9)\n\nWhat’s missing or weak (not fulfilled or partial):\n- Model configuration (Req 4): The code intends to use ImplicitMF with n_factors=20, lambda=0.01, n_epochs=15, but the actual ImplicitMF initialization uses epochs=1 and the per-epoch loop doesn’t actually retrain (no incremental training). This breaks the requirement of a fixed 15 training epochs and deterministic per-run initialization across seeds. Recommend: pass n_epochs=15 and implement a proper per-epoch training loop or rely on LensKit’s built-in iterative training. Ensure that the seed controls the initialization RNG (e.g., set numpy.random.seed and lenskit’s RNG state before training).\n- Results logging and reporting (Req 7): The spec requires a results_seeded.csv with columns: seed, K, metric_name, value. The code only writes a summary_seeded.csv and stores a numpy blob. It does not generate the per-run per-K metric rows in the required CSV format. Recommendation: generate a proper long-form CSV named results_seeded.csv with rows for each seed, each K, each metric (NDCG or Recall). Also compute and export a summary file with mean and std across seeds (Req 6, 7).\n- Aggregation and statistics (Req 6): While per-seed metrics are stored, there is no computation of mean and std across seeds, nor a per-metric table summarizing results. Implement: after all seeds, compute mean and std for each (metric, K) and write a summary file, then log seed-wise comparisons.\n- Reproducibility hygiene (Req 8): Some randomness is controlled, but the implementation uses a custom per-user seed derivation. It would be cleaner to also set Python’s random.seed and ensure all sources of randomness (numpy, Python’s random, LensKit RNG) are explicitly seeded and captured in logs.\n- Edge-case handling (Req 9): Some users might end up with no test items or training items after splitting if thresholds vary. The code currently drops users with fewer than min_interactions in the original data; add explicit checks after split to drop users with zero train or zero test interactions and log these cases.\n- Documentation and configurability (Req 10): The config section is implicit; consider adding a small CLI/config mechanism (argparse or YAML) to set seeds, f, alpha, hyperparameters, and K values, plus a short README-style docstring inside the script explaining experiment design.\n\nConcrete suggestions to raise to fully compliant: \n- Align model config with requirements: instantiate ImplicitMF with n_factors=20, reg=0.01, epochs=15, and ensure deterministic initialization. Replace the per-epoch dummy loop with either a proper training loop or simple evaluation after training.\n- Implement proper results logging: generate and save a CSV at results_seeded.csv with columns [seed, K, metric_name, value], and compute per-metric means and std across seeds in a separate summary file (summary_seeded.csv or similar).\n- Compute and log seed-wise run times and other metadata (start time, end time) for traceability.\n- Improve robustness: after performing per-user split, verify that there are both train and test interactions per user; drop the problematic users and record justification in logs.\n- Improve documentation: add a short README-style block that explains seeds rationale, the split strategy, and how to reproduce results.\n\nIf you’d like, I can provide a revised, drop-in version that fixes the above and produces the requested outputs.\n                ', 'Feedback about execution time': '', 'Instructions': {'Response format': 'Your response should be a brief outline/sketch of your proposed solution in natural language (7-10 sent